 This 14.42% gap suggests the model memorized some training patterns rather than fully generalizing, though it remains acceptable for our medical problem.

 ---
# Try to enhancment the model : 
To improve the initial model's recall for heart disease cases, several hyperparameters were adjusted:

1. **Increased n_estimators**: From 100 to 200 trees for better ensemble learning
2. **Reduced learning_rate**: From 0.1 to 0.05 for more gradual and precise learning
3. **Increased max_depth**: From 5 to 6 to capture more complex patterns
4. **Added subsampling**: Set subsample=0.8 and colsample_bytree=0.8 to reduce overfitting
5. **Optimized class weighting**: Increased scale_pos_weight from default to 10 to prioritize minority class (heart disease patients)

This resulted in improved recall from 54% to 84% for detecting heart disease cases.
---


model = xgb.XGBClassifier(
    n_estimators=200,         
    learning_rate=0.05,        
    max_depth=6,               
    min_child_weight=1,
    subsample=0.8,
    colsample_bytree=0.8,
    scale_pos_weight=10,       
    random_state=42
)

model.fit(X_train_res, y_train_res)
y_pred = model.predict(X_test)

accuracy = accuracy_score(y_test, y_pred)
print(f"\n Accuracy {accuracy:.2%}")
print("\n Report")
print(classification_report(y_test, y_pred))
| Model| scale_pos_weight |Accuracy | Recall  |Precision (patient)|Description|
| -----| -----------------|---------|---------|-------------------|-----------|
| 1st  | default (1)      | 85%     |   54%   |      32%          |Failure to detect patients|
| 2nd  |    10            | 60%     |   91%   |     18%           |Focus on discovering patients|

Results: Accuracy dropped to 59.84%, but recall for heart disease cases surged to 91%, 
successfully identifying most at-risk patients at the cost of increased false positives.
